{
  "dom_id": "step-q10832",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}-x$ at the point $(1,3).$",
  "worked_solution": "Let $f(x)=\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}-x.$ Computing the derivative ${f}^{\u2032}(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =-12{x}^{-4}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute ${f}^{\u2032}(1)$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(1)=-12(1)^{-4}-1=-13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=-13$ and the coordinates of our point $(1,3),$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(3) & =-13(x-1) \\\\ y & =-13x+13+3 \\\\ y & =-13x+16.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
